/usr/examples -- small programs to read, run and change

Copy one to your own directory first:  cp /usr/examples/hello.c .

  hello.c         the classic first C program
                    cc hello.c; ./a.out
  multitask.c     several processes at once: workers that never yield,
                  interleaved by the timer (preemption), each with its own
                  memory, results through a pipe, exit statuses by wait()
                    cc -o multitask multitask.c; ./multitask [workers]
  draw.c          a picture on the video card's screen: lines, boxes, a
                  filled block, text, through /dev/video (man 4 video);
                  the console's text comes back when it ends
                    cc -o draw draw.c; ./draw
  clock.c         a clock, drawn with the graphics library (man gfx):
                  circles, lines, text, and hands that move by being
                  drawn twice in the ink that turns dots over
                    cc -o clock clock.c -lgfx; ./clock
  mouse.c         the mouse: a cross that follows it, the left button
                  draws, the right one ends (man gfx, man 4 mouse)
                    cc -o mouse mouse.c -lgfx; ./mouse
  flight.c        a very small flight simulator, drawn in lines: the
                  horizon, the ground, an airfield to take off from
                  (hangars, a terminal, a tower), a city, hills and
                  another airfield to find, over a panel of six
                  instruments; the mouse is the stick (or the arrow
                  keys), and there are three ways of changing the
                  picture to compare (man gfx, man 4 video)
                    cc -o flight flight.c -lgfx; ./flight
  wm.hand         a hand for the window manager to play: two terminals,
                  a command in each, BASIC drawing in a window, one
                  dragged, one closed, programs started with win; then
                  the desk's own: Files, a file opened in view, the
                  Windows menu, the Clock, a Snapshot; a terminal made
                  smaller, and bigger with vi in it; Files made smaller
                  (man wm)
                    wm -s wm.hand
  finder.hand     a hand for the finder to play, in a directory it
                  makes (finder.d): a file picked, renamed and copied
                  from the File menu, a folder made and gone into, a
                  file moved into it, a directory removed, the clock
                  started from the Apps menu, a file opened in view
                  (man finder)
                    wm -s finder.hand
  edit.hand       a hand for the editor to play, on a file it makes
                  (notes): three lines typed, the cursor placed with
                  the mouse and a word put in, a line cut and pasted
                  from the Edit menu, a word found, the file saved
                  from the File menu (man edit)
                    wm -s edit.hand
  paint.hand      a hand for paint to play, on a drawing it makes
                  (draw1): a line, a box, a filled oval in a pattern,
                  a line with the pen, an oval drawn and taken back,
                  the drawing saved, and saved as a picture (pic.pbm)
                  (man paint)
                    wm -s paint.hand
  sheet.hand      a hand for the spreadsheet to play, on a sheet it
                  makes (budget): four cells of text and three numbers
                  typed, a sum; a formula put in after a press of the
                  mouse on a cell; another copied and pasted from the
                  Edit menu; the sheet saved, and printed by sheet -p
                  (man sheet)
                    wm -s sheet.hand
  people.c        C and the SQL library: reads the "shop" database
                  that inventory.bas makes (before its last line drops it)
                    cc -o people people.c -lsql; ./people
  inventory.bas   BASIC and SQL: a table made, filled, queried, an SQL
                  error caught with ON ERROR
                    basic inventory.bas
  plot.bas        BASIC's graphics: a curve with its axes and labels,
                  a ball of ellipses, on the video card's screen
                    basic plot.bas
  primes.bas      BASIC: the primes below 1000 (a sieve, PRINT USING)
                    basic primes.bas
  guess.bas       BASIC: a guessing game (INPUT, RND)
                    basic guess.bas
  count.sh        a shell script: for, expr, if / test
                    sh count.sh one two three

More come as the system grows. The manual explains each language and
command: man cc, man basic, man sql, man sh (man intro for the rest).
